1
00:00:25,959 --> 00:00:28,160
♪ ♪

2
00:00:36,503 --> 00:00:38,336
♪ ♪

3
00:00:49,416 --> 00:00:51,450
♪ ♪

4
00:01:05,999 --> 00:01:07,933
♪ ♪

5
00:01:38,031 --> 00:01:39,965
♪ ♪

6
00:02:09,997 --> 00:02:11,930
♪ ♪

7
00:02:30,183 --> 00:02:31,549
Evening, Judge Franklin.

8
00:02:31,618 --> 00:02:34,178
Mayor Barelli is expecting you.

9
00:02:54,908 --> 00:02:56,175
Evening, Mr. Brennert.

10
00:02:56,243 --> 00:02:58,443
The mayor is waiting for you.

11
00:03:27,006 --> 00:03:29,352
Why do I have to fight so hard

12
00:03:29,376 --> 00:03:31,721
to get a little bit of
cooperation around here?

13
00:03:31,745 --> 00:03:34,679
These...

14
00:03:37,817 --> 00:03:39,084
Think you can handle this.

15
00:03:39,153 --> 00:03:40,284
Welcome, Judge.

16
00:03:40,353 --> 00:03:41,853
It's good to have you here.

17
00:03:41,922 --> 00:03:43,065
Please take a seat.

18
00:03:43,089 --> 00:03:44,367
Thanks for coming
by. Take a seat.

19
00:03:44,391 --> 00:03:45,735
Gentlemen, I'll see you later.

20
00:03:50,063 --> 00:03:51,983
You betrayed me! Betrayed what?

21
00:03:52,032 --> 00:03:53,175
Betrayed what?

22
00:03:53,199 --> 00:03:54,744
Look, you stand up like a man!

23
00:03:58,137 --> 00:04:01,406
Hey, don't talk to me like that!

24
00:04:01,475 --> 00:04:03,775
Hey, you!

25
00:04:03,844 --> 00:04:05,244
Get him off of me! Get him off!

26
00:04:19,493 --> 00:04:22,053
All right, O'Neill,
time to come out.

27
00:04:27,167 --> 00:04:29,434
Hi, guys.

28
00:04:33,206 --> 00:04:35,273
Hey, let's not get cute.

29
00:04:45,819 --> 00:04:48,887
Book him.

30
00:04:50,290 --> 00:04:52,223
Come on, let's go.

31
00:05:18,819 --> 00:05:20,964
I heard you spent
the weekend in jail.

32
00:05:20,988 --> 00:05:22,253
You heard right,

33
00:05:22,322 --> 00:05:25,356
because nobody
showed to make my bail.

34
00:05:25,425 --> 00:05:26,970
I did nine days once.

35
00:05:26,994 --> 00:05:28,426
'60s, Alabama.

36
00:05:28,495 --> 00:05:31,195
Never occurred to me that
the paper would post bail.

37
00:05:31,264 --> 00:05:32,976
And I was on assignment.

38
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:34,143
You weren't.

39
00:05:35,435 --> 00:05:37,969
You think I'd go to the
mayor's mansion for fun?

40
00:05:38,037 --> 00:05:39,303
I had a tip.

41
00:05:39,372 --> 00:05:41,451
Heard Mayor Barelli was
having an emergency meeting.

42
00:05:41,475 --> 00:05:43,475
I thought it might
have something to do

43
00:05:43,543 --> 00:05:45,287
with the senate seat
he's been eyeing.

44
00:05:45,311 --> 00:05:46,591
So, did it?

45
00:05:46,646 --> 00:05:48,090
Well, Judge Franklin
and Jack Brennert

46
00:05:48,114 --> 00:05:49,247
were the only ones there.

47
00:05:49,315 --> 00:05:51,149
Well, you've been baptized, kid.

48
00:05:51,218 --> 00:05:53,752
Never knew a reporter worth
his byline hasn't been busted

49
00:05:53,820 --> 00:05:55,153
chasing a story.

50
00:05:55,221 --> 00:05:56,565
Here's looking at you.

51
00:05:56,589 --> 00:05:58,356
Well, those three
may go way back,

52
00:05:58,424 --> 00:06:00,191
but it was far from a reunion.

53
00:06:00,260 --> 00:06:02,560
The mayor almost put his
fist down Brennert's throat.

54
00:06:02,629 --> 00:06:05,096
Brennert almost put the
mayor through the French doors.

55
00:06:05,165 --> 00:06:07,699
Franklin looked like he
wanted to strangle both of them.

56
00:06:07,768 --> 00:06:09,311
Oh, what were
they fighting about?

57
00:06:09,335 --> 00:06:10,635
I couldn't follow up.

58
00:06:10,703 --> 00:06:11,903
I was in jail.

59
00:06:11,972 --> 00:06:14,516
Get any pictures?

60
00:06:14,540 --> 00:06:16,975
Cops got your camera.

61
00:06:22,415 --> 00:06:24,482
Is the mayor having a
press conference today?

62
00:06:24,551 --> 00:06:25,917
Yeah, in the rotunda.

63
00:06:25,986 --> 00:06:28,019
Couple of minutes.

64
00:06:28,087 --> 00:06:29,420
Read that.

65
00:06:31,458 --> 00:06:33,778
Hi. This is Paul O'Neill,
<i>The Chicago Daily Examiner.</i>

66
00:06:33,827 --> 00:06:35,660
I need to speak to the mayor.

67
00:06:35,729 --> 00:06:37,496
It's urgent that I speak to him!

68
00:06:37,564 --> 00:06:39,264
Oh, my God.

69
00:06:43,337 --> 00:06:45,470
They already left.

70
00:06:45,539 --> 00:06:47,004
You call the police.

71
00:06:47,073 --> 00:06:49,441
I'm on my way.

72
00:06:52,846 --> 00:06:54,078
Yeah, hello, Sergeant.

73
00:06:54,147 --> 00:06:55,480
This is Jim Milburn.

74
00:06:55,549 --> 00:06:57,682
Hey, we just got a tip.

75
00:06:57,751 --> 00:06:59,951
Some guy says he's
gonna kill the mayor.

76
00:07:01,455 --> 00:07:02,654
Um, ladies and gentlemen.

77
00:07:02,723 --> 00:07:05,157
Ladies and gentlemen, may
I have your attention, please?

78
00:07:05,225 --> 00:07:07,392
The mayor will make
a brief statement.

79
00:07:07,461 --> 00:07:09,861
He'll answer your
questions when he's through.

80
00:07:12,966 --> 00:07:14,098
Thank you.

81
00:07:14,167 --> 00:07:15,901
Thank you and welcome.

82
00:07:15,969 --> 00:07:18,169
In a little less than a year,

83
00:07:18,237 --> 00:07:20,404
the people of this
state must decide

84
00:07:20,473 --> 00:07:23,608
who will represent them in
the United States Senate.

85
00:07:23,676 --> 00:07:25,177
They will either

86
00:07:25,245 --> 00:07:30,182
decide for six more
years of the same thing,

87
00:07:30,250 --> 00:07:32,918
of if they agree with me,

88
00:07:32,986 --> 00:07:35,453
they'll decide that
it's time for a change.

89
00:07:35,522 --> 00:07:36,821
Time for a new agenda.

90
00:07:36,890 --> 00:07:39,890
Time for a new
voice to be heard.

91
00:07:39,959 --> 00:07:42,561
Time for a new
generation to be heard.

92
00:07:42,629 --> 00:07:46,364
I stand before you today
as I start down that long road,

93
00:07:46,433 --> 00:07:49,567
confident that it will end...

94
00:07:49,636 --> 00:07:51,556
Everybody, get down!
With a seat in the United...

95
00:07:54,007 --> 00:07:55,073
Look, there he is!

96
00:07:55,909 --> 00:07:56,842
Hey! Hey...!

97
00:07:56,910 --> 00:07:58,054
After him!

98
00:07:58,078 --> 00:07:59,255
Come on, let's go!

99
00:07:59,279 --> 00:08:00,456
There he is up there!

100
00:08:02,082 --> 00:08:04,048
There he goes!

101
00:08:04,117 --> 00:08:05,783
Get around the other end.

102
00:08:05,852 --> 00:08:07,218
Hold it right there!

103
00:08:09,756 --> 00:08:10,955
Get out of the way!

104
00:08:11,024 --> 00:08:13,458
Clear the area.

105
00:08:13,527 --> 00:08:14,527
Halt!

106
00:08:16,529 --> 00:08:19,089
He's in here.

107
00:08:20,733 --> 00:08:22,013
Watch out!

108
00:08:22,068 --> 00:08:23,434
Go to the other staircase.

109
00:08:23,503 --> 00:08:24,902
Surround the second floor.

110
00:08:24,971 --> 00:08:27,605
Try to get around
the east end there.

111
00:08:29,209 --> 00:08:31,876
All right, stand back.

112
00:08:31,945 --> 00:08:33,289
Stand back. Let him through!

113
00:08:33,313 --> 00:08:35,191
Go get those people back
there... Coming through.

114
00:08:35,215 --> 00:08:36,347
Hey, get out of the way.

115
00:08:36,416 --> 00:08:37,993
Look, we got to
have some room here.

116
00:08:38,017 --> 00:08:39,762
You got to transport
him as soon as possible.

117
00:08:39,786 --> 00:08:41,786
Hold it right there.
Hold it right there.

118
00:08:43,022 --> 00:08:44,934
There's something
I want you to see.

119
00:08:44,958 --> 00:08:48,170
Hey, take him out. Let's go!

120
00:08:48,194 --> 00:08:49,906
Come on. Keep 'em back.

121
00:08:54,368 --> 00:08:56,178
We got some tape on the shooter.

122
00:08:56,202 --> 00:08:58,013
I want you to look at it.

123
00:08:58,037 --> 00:09:00,271
Go ahead.

124
00:09:00,340 --> 00:09:01,906
Freeze it.

125
00:09:04,044 --> 00:09:06,444
What do you think?

126
00:09:06,513 --> 00:09:07,757
Wait a minute.

127
00:09:07,781 --> 00:09:09,246
I know him.

128
00:09:09,315 --> 00:09:13,251
That's the guy from the
nuclear demonstrations.

129
00:09:13,319 --> 00:09:17,567
Paul, didn't you write
a piece on the guy

130
00:09:17,591 --> 00:09:19,090
that led those demonstrations?

131
00:09:19,159 --> 00:09:20,258
Yeah.

132
00:09:20,326 --> 00:09:22,005
Weren't you getting
letters from him?

133
00:09:22,029 --> 00:09:23,940
Yeah, I was. All right.

134
00:09:23,964 --> 00:09:25,363
Some reporter called the police.

135
00:09:25,431 --> 00:09:26,598
Now, how did he know

136
00:09:26,666 --> 00:09:28,766
there was gonna be an
attempt on the mayor?

137
00:09:28,835 --> 00:09:29,934
Was there a call?

138
00:09:30,003 --> 00:09:31,603
Another letter?

139
00:09:31,671 --> 00:09:33,838
A letter.

140
00:09:33,907 --> 00:09:36,207
Let's have it.

141
00:09:40,947 --> 00:09:42,914
There's no name.

142
00:09:42,983 --> 00:09:44,315
What's his name?

143
00:09:44,384 --> 00:09:46,117
Lou, I can't do that.

144
00:09:49,189 --> 00:09:50,955
Let's take a walk.

145
00:09:55,895 --> 00:09:57,261
Look, Paul,

146
00:09:57,330 --> 00:09:59,897
I know some day you're
gonna want to grow up

147
00:09:59,966 --> 00:10:02,667
and win a Pulitzer Prize,

148
00:10:02,736 --> 00:10:04,980
but this isn't just
some two-bit snitch

149
00:10:05,004 --> 00:10:06,181
you're protecting.

150
00:10:06,205 --> 00:10:08,939
This is a stone-cold killer

151
00:10:09,008 --> 00:10:12,109
who shot down the mayor
of this city in broad daylight.

152
00:10:12,178 --> 00:10:13,889
Lou... Now, listen to me, man.

153
00:10:13,913 --> 00:10:15,658
You cannot hold out on me.

154
00:10:15,682 --> 00:10:17,648
Not now, not this time.

155
00:10:20,152 --> 00:10:22,587
All right, but I'm
in on the bust.

156
00:10:24,524 --> 00:10:26,290
Deal.

157
00:10:26,359 --> 00:10:28,393
I met this guy about
six months ago.

158
00:10:28,461 --> 00:10:30,341
He's an activist, but
he's strictly non-violent.

159
00:10:30,397 --> 00:10:32,162
I mean, none of
this weird stuff...

160
00:10:32,231 --> 00:10:33,431
Uh, politics, the environment.

161
00:10:33,500 --> 00:10:34,643
He's involved. He's committed.

162
00:10:34,667 --> 00:10:35,811
Paul, what's his name?!

163
00:10:35,835 --> 00:10:36,934
I like this guy.

164
00:10:37,003 --> 00:10:38,247
So what?!

165
00:10:38,271 --> 00:10:39,937
His name is Chris Reardon.

166
00:10:40,006 --> 00:10:42,139
He always wears pretty
much the same thing...

167
00:10:42,209 --> 00:10:46,744
Cubs baseball cap,
army jacket, sunglasses.

168
00:10:46,813 --> 00:10:47,912
Bingo.

169
00:10:56,423 --> 00:10:58,355
All right, look around.

170
00:11:19,146 --> 00:11:21,706
I told you to wait outside.

171
00:11:28,788 --> 00:11:30,921
Typefaces match.

172
00:11:34,961 --> 00:11:36,873
What the hell is
going on here? Freeze!

173
00:11:36,897 --> 00:11:39,564
Hey... get your hands off of me!

174
00:11:39,632 --> 00:11:41,332
Do I have you to thank for this,

175
00:11:41,401 --> 00:11:42,778
O'Neill? Will you
let me talk to him?

176
00:11:42,802 --> 00:11:43,735
Forget it.

177
00:11:43,803 --> 00:11:45,013
We have a warrant
for your arrest.

178
00:11:45,037 --> 00:11:46,571
For what? Attempted murder.

179
00:11:46,639 --> 00:11:48,750
What the hell are
you talking about?

180
00:11:48,774 --> 00:11:50,275
Somebody just shot the mayor.

181
00:11:50,343 --> 00:11:51,842
Well, it wasn't me!

182
00:11:51,911 --> 00:11:53,378
But whoever did it succeeded.

183
00:11:53,447 --> 00:11:55,558
He just died on
the operating table.

184
00:11:55,582 --> 00:11:56,862
Cuff him.

185
00:11:57,750 --> 00:11:58,682
Let's go.

186
00:11:58,751 --> 00:12:00,151
O'Neill, do something.

187
00:12:00,220 --> 00:12:01,230
Come on, move it.

188
00:12:01,254 --> 00:12:03,814
O'Neill!

189
00:12:04,724 --> 00:12:07,291
You know I wouldn't
do something like this.

190
00:12:07,360 --> 00:12:08,492
O'Neill!

191
00:12:26,178 --> 00:12:27,678
Excuse me.

192
00:12:27,747 --> 00:12:30,715
Uh... Excuse me.

193
00:12:30,783 --> 00:12:33,584
Uh... Uh, excuse me.

194
00:12:33,653 --> 00:12:35,086
Uh, excuse me.

195
00:12:35,154 --> 00:12:36,588
I'll call you back.

196
00:12:36,656 --> 00:12:38,534
Mr. Matlock? Yeah.

197
00:12:38,558 --> 00:12:40,336
Paul O'Neill,<i> Chicago
Daily Examiner.</i>

198
00:12:40,360 --> 00:12:42,038
Uh-huh. Well, let
me take this for you.

199
00:12:42,062 --> 00:12:43,161
Oh, thank you.

200
00:12:43,229 --> 00:12:44,740
Good, you came
straight from the airport.

201
00:12:44,764 --> 00:12:46,030
Yeah.

202
00:12:46,099 --> 00:12:49,000
The wire I got from Harry
sounded pretty urgent.

203
00:12:49,068 --> 00:12:50,634
Uh, how is Harry?

204
00:12:50,703 --> 00:12:52,281
Working hard? Yes, sir.

205
00:12:52,305 --> 00:12:53,415
Uh, Harry Eubanks

206
00:12:53,439 --> 00:12:54,850
is the hardest-working
publisher I know.

207
00:12:54,874 --> 00:12:55,885
That's why we're number one.

208
00:12:55,909 --> 00:12:56,974
Yeah, yeah.

209
00:12:57,043 --> 00:13:00,244
Well, uh... tell me
about this Chris, uh...

210
00:13:00,313 --> 00:13:01,590
uh, Weir... Reardon.

211
00:13:01,614 --> 00:13:02,680
Reardon, yeah.

212
00:13:02,748 --> 00:13:04,860
Well, around here,
everybody loved Brian Barelli,

213
00:13:04,884 --> 00:13:06,762
which means nobody
likes Chris Reardon.

214
00:13:06,786 --> 00:13:08,764
It's a miracle he
hasn't been lynched.

215
00:13:08,788 --> 00:13:11,255
Lynched? Mr. Matlock,
I was right there.

216
00:13:11,324 --> 00:13:12,802
I saw the guy who
shot Mayor Barelli.

217
00:13:12,826 --> 00:13:14,737
He looked like Chris Reardon...
He even dressed like him...

218
00:13:14,761 --> 00:13:16,093
But I don't think it was him.

219
00:13:16,162 --> 00:13:17,874
I'm almost sure it wasn't him.

220
00:13:17,898 --> 00:13:19,175
You know this fellow? Yeah.

221
00:13:19,199 --> 00:13:20,130
I know him.

222
00:13:20,200 --> 00:13:21,365
They got him right up here.

223
00:13:21,434 --> 00:13:22,767
Huh.

224
00:13:29,809 --> 00:13:30,975
You believe

225
00:13:31,044 --> 00:13:33,544
in the system of justice,
don't you, Mr. Matlock?

226
00:13:33,613 --> 00:13:35,913
Of course.

227
00:13:35,982 --> 00:13:39,383
Now, the first thing
we have to establish

228
00:13:39,452 --> 00:13:42,353
is where you were at
the time of the murder.

229
00:13:42,422 --> 00:13:45,155
You realize that the
entire legal process

230
00:13:45,224 --> 00:13:46,724
was created by the rich,

231
00:13:46,793 --> 00:13:48,526
for the rich, so...

232
00:13:48,594 --> 00:13:50,561
there's no real justice.

233
00:13:50,630 --> 00:13:53,831
Uh, Mr. Reardon,
I can't defend you

234
00:13:53,900 --> 00:13:56,167
unless you answer my questions.

235
00:13:56,236 --> 00:13:59,137
Now, I need to
know where you were

236
00:13:59,205 --> 00:14:00,783
at the time of the murder,

237
00:14:00,807 --> 00:14:03,407
who could have
gotten into your home,

238
00:14:03,476 --> 00:14:04,742
used your typewriter.

239
00:14:04,811 --> 00:14:07,478
If you won't cooperate with me,

240
00:14:07,547 --> 00:14:10,381
you could wind up in
Joliet for the rest of your life.

241
00:14:10,450 --> 00:14:12,216
Mr. Matlock, I'm going to jail.

242
00:14:12,284 --> 00:14:14,162
And not even you
can change that.

243
00:14:14,186 --> 00:14:16,520
Because I'm poor.

244
00:14:16,589 --> 00:14:19,490
Because I protest
society's corruption.

245
00:14:19,558 --> 00:14:23,360
Because the rich and powerful
want to silence my voice.

246
00:14:23,429 --> 00:14:24,996
That's where it's at.

247
00:14:28,434 --> 00:14:29,434
Fine.

248
00:14:30,603 --> 00:14:32,136
Mr. Reardon,

249
00:14:32,205 --> 00:14:33,882
you're a bright man, I suppose,

250
00:14:33,906 --> 00:14:36,285
but when your
next lawyer visits,

251
00:14:36,309 --> 00:14:38,876
I'd get off my soapbox
and talk about the case.

252
00:14:38,944 --> 00:14:40,155
Guard!

253
00:14:40,179 --> 00:14:41,179
All right.

254
00:14:42,749 --> 00:14:45,983
Not that I think it
will help much...

255
00:14:46,052 --> 00:14:47,986
but I'll tell you.

256
00:15:01,300 --> 00:15:02,767
Gonna take the case?

257
00:15:02,836 --> 00:15:04,035
Yeah.

258
00:15:04,104 --> 00:15:05,236
Only because,

259
00:15:05,304 --> 00:15:07,972
in all the years I've
known Harry Eubanks,

260
00:15:08,041 --> 00:15:09,506
he's never asked a favor,

261
00:15:09,576 --> 00:15:11,308
so I got to assume my defending

262
00:15:11,377 --> 00:15:12,943
some aging, bigmouthed hippie

263
00:15:13,012 --> 00:15:14,712
is damned important to him.

264
00:15:14,781 --> 00:15:18,516
Harry Eubanks didn't
send that telegram.

265
00:15:18,585 --> 00:15:20,718
I did.

266
00:15:22,222 --> 00:15:25,222
The fact is, Harry Eubanks
thinks Chris Reardon did it,

267
00:15:25,291 --> 00:15:26,702
but when I heard that you two

268
00:15:26,726 --> 00:15:27,870
are old friends, I...

269
00:15:27,894 --> 00:15:30,306
I had to send that telegram.

270
00:15:30,330 --> 00:15:32,897
Chris doesn't have a prayer
without a good attorney.

271
00:15:34,768 --> 00:15:36,601
You could still back out.

272
00:15:37,703 --> 00:15:39,504
It would prejudice his case.

273
00:15:42,175 --> 00:15:44,642
You got me up
here to get a story.

274
00:15:44,711 --> 00:15:46,177
No, I didn't.

275
00:15:46,245 --> 00:15:48,178
Well, then, why?
Because I had to.

276
00:15:48,247 --> 00:15:50,126
Why? Because I
don't think he's guilty.

277
00:15:50,150 --> 00:15:51,150
You don't?

278
00:15:51,217 --> 00:15:52,650
I saw the killer tear his jacket

279
00:15:52,718 --> 00:15:54,330
when he left the
scene of the crime.

280
00:15:54,354 --> 00:15:55,698
Chris Reardon's
jacket wasn't torn.

281
00:15:55,722 --> 00:15:56,999
And unlike practically
everybody else

282
00:15:57,023 --> 00:15:59,157
around here, Chris
Reardon never sold out.

283
00:15:59,225 --> 00:16:00,636
He's still fighting
the good fight.

284
00:16:00,660 --> 00:16:01,660
I... I like that.

285
00:16:02,828 --> 00:16:05,388
And because somebody
used me to frame him.

286
00:16:06,366 --> 00:16:07,731
I don't like that.

287
00:16:09,102 --> 00:16:10,801
You own your own house?

288
00:16:10,870 --> 00:16:12,637
Such as it is.

289
00:16:13,739 --> 00:16:16,073
Get ready to transfer the title.

290
00:16:16,142 --> 00:16:18,442
Somebody's going to pay my fee.

291
00:16:24,750 --> 00:16:28,652
<i>In view of the</i> heinous
nature of this crime

292
00:16:28,721 --> 00:16:31,689
and the substantial
possibility of flight,

293
00:16:31,758 --> 00:16:33,958
bail is set in the amount

294
00:16:34,026 --> 00:16:35,893
of $1 million. Who the hell

295
00:16:35,962 --> 00:16:37,907
has $1 million? Chris, please,
please. Uh, Your Honor...

296
00:16:37,931 --> 00:16:39,508
$1 million, sir.

297
00:16:39,532 --> 00:16:41,410
What the hell... Chris,
Chris, I'll talk to him.

298
00:16:41,434 --> 00:16:42,799
I'll talk to him.

299
00:16:44,270 --> 00:16:47,872
I understand I have
you to thank for, uh, him.

300
00:16:47,940 --> 00:16:48,973
Yeah.

301
00:16:49,041 --> 00:16:50,519
I feel badly about this. Good.

302
00:16:50,543 --> 00:16:53,744
You ought to.

303
00:16:53,813 --> 00:16:55,324
Uh, Mr. Matlock? Hmm?

304
00:16:55,348 --> 00:16:56,780
Before you talk to that judge,

305
00:16:56,849 --> 00:16:58,816
there are a few things
you should know.

306
00:17:00,019 --> 00:17:02,579
Come in.

307
00:17:03,055 --> 00:17:04,255
You got a minute, Your Honor?

308
00:17:05,391 --> 00:17:07,402
One minute is about
all I have, counselor.

309
00:17:07,426 --> 00:17:08,359
Thank you.

310
00:17:08,427 --> 00:17:09,872
And if it is about the case,

311
00:17:09,896 --> 00:17:11,573
we should call the
assistant state's attorney.

312
00:17:11,597 --> 00:17:12,808
Oh, that's up to you.

313
00:17:12,832 --> 00:17:15,633
I'm here because it's
come to my attention

314
00:17:15,701 --> 00:17:18,970
that you and the late Mayor
Barelli were old friends.

315
00:17:20,440 --> 00:17:22,351
You're concerned
about conflict of interest.

316
00:17:22,375 --> 00:17:24,053
As a matter of fact, I am.

317
00:17:24,077 --> 00:17:25,476
Don't be.

318
00:17:25,545 --> 00:17:27,523
I won't be sitting
on Reardon's trial.

319
00:17:27,547 --> 00:17:30,214
I only sat on his,
uh, bail hearing

320
00:17:30,282 --> 00:17:32,149
because Judge Powell was ill.

321
00:17:32,218 --> 00:17:36,754
You can appeal the amount,
but you won't do any better.

322
00:17:36,823 --> 00:17:40,057
Brian Barelli had
many old friends.

323
00:17:42,929 --> 00:17:45,262
I'm due in court,
uh, Mr. Matlock,

324
00:17:45,331 --> 00:17:47,398
so you'll excuse me.

325
00:18:02,848 --> 00:18:03,848
Excuse me.

326
00:18:03,917 --> 00:18:05,027
Here's the stuff you wanted.

327
00:18:05,051 --> 00:18:06,195
Oh, thanks. It's the pictures.

328
00:18:06,219 --> 00:18:08,352
And-and that's where
the killer was standing?

329
00:18:08,421 --> 00:18:10,981
Yeah, it was right up there.

330
00:18:11,691 --> 00:18:13,123
Come on down, Michelle.

331
00:18:16,128 --> 00:18:17,473
You know her? Yeah.

332
00:18:17,497 --> 00:18:19,129
That's Michelle
Thomas, my associate.

333
00:18:19,199 --> 00:18:20,342
Is she married?

334
00:18:20,366 --> 00:18:21,810
No. Engaged?

335
00:18:21,834 --> 00:18:22,766
No.

336
00:18:22,835 --> 00:18:24,968
Living with anybody?

337
00:18:25,037 --> 00:18:26,404
No.

338
00:18:27,507 --> 00:18:28,606
Damn.

339
00:18:42,688 --> 00:18:44,255
Oh, here we go.

340
00:18:44,323 --> 00:18:45,701
So, you think the killer

341
00:18:45,725 --> 00:18:47,136
was hired by one
of these people.

342
00:18:47,160 --> 00:18:48,092
Well, the press conference

343
00:18:48,161 --> 00:18:49,304
wasn't called until
the last minute.

344
00:18:49,328 --> 00:18:50,705
According to my source
in the mayor's office,

345
00:18:50,729 --> 00:18:52,240
these people were
the only ones who knew

346
00:18:52,264 --> 00:18:53,775
when and where it
was going to be called.

347
00:18:53,799 --> 00:18:55,711
Hmm. Well, that's
Judge Stuart Franklin.

348
00:18:55,735 --> 00:18:57,535
Ex-cop, ex-city councilman.

349
00:18:57,604 --> 00:19:00,164
Good friend of Mayor Barelli.

350
00:19:01,007 --> 00:19:02,906
I don't recognize this guy.

351
00:19:02,975 --> 00:19:04,253
That's Jack Brennert.

352
00:19:04,277 --> 00:19:05,854
He owns a couple of factories.

353
00:19:05,878 --> 00:19:07,311
Good friend? Supposedly.

354
00:19:07,379 --> 00:19:09,079
Why supposedly?

355
00:19:09,148 --> 00:19:10,759
Because a couple of
days before the murder,

356
00:19:10,783 --> 00:19:11,793
Brennert, Franklin and Barelli

357
00:19:11,817 --> 00:19:13,429
got into a serious
shoving match.

358
00:19:13,453 --> 00:19:14,385
What about?

359
00:19:14,454 --> 00:19:15,853
I don't know.

360
00:19:17,590 --> 00:19:19,301
Is that Mrs. Barelli?

361
00:19:19,325 --> 00:19:20,669
Mm-hmm.

362
00:19:20,693 --> 00:19:22,671
That's Joseph Chan,
Barelli's right-hand man.

363
00:19:22,695 --> 00:19:25,162
What about this girl press aide?

364
00:19:25,231 --> 00:19:26,497
Melissa Landis.

365
00:19:26,565 --> 00:19:27,798
She's my source.

366
00:19:27,867 --> 00:19:29,166
Ah.

367
00:19:29,235 --> 00:19:32,169
Well, I guess we can
start with these four.

368
00:19:32,238 --> 00:19:33,370
Yeah.

369
00:19:33,439 --> 00:19:35,039
I'll get Tyler.

370
00:19:53,393 --> 00:19:54,903
Maybe we got a
problem underneath.

371
00:19:54,927 --> 00:19:56,671
Montero, look at
pump number one.

372
00:19:56,695 --> 00:19:58,161
Got it.

373
00:19:58,230 --> 00:20:00,597
I'll check up here.

374
00:20:11,811 --> 00:20:13,744
Check number two pump!

375
00:20:13,813 --> 00:20:15,045
Mr. Brennert?

376
00:20:15,114 --> 00:20:16,725
Yeah! I'm Ben Matlock,

377
00:20:16,749 --> 00:20:17,882
Chris Reardon's lawyer.

378
00:20:17,950 --> 00:20:19,895
I'd like to ask you
a couple questions.

379
00:20:19,919 --> 00:20:20,919
Come on.

380
00:20:27,993 --> 00:20:30,194
That's a hard
hat area out there.

381
00:20:30,263 --> 00:20:32,262
I didn't have one.

382
00:20:32,331 --> 00:20:33,363
Right, I need this.

383
00:20:33,432 --> 00:20:35,010
Some hotshot lawyer
gets hurt outside

384
00:20:35,034 --> 00:20:36,466
and sues me out of business.

385
00:20:36,535 --> 00:20:37,868
What's your problem, huh?

386
00:20:37,937 --> 00:20:39,136
Defending a lunatic

387
00:20:39,204 --> 00:20:41,304
like Reardon doesn't
keep you busy enough?

388
00:20:41,373 --> 00:20:42,873
What do you want with me?

389
00:20:42,942 --> 00:20:45,876
I understand you and the
mayor had quite a tussle

390
00:20:45,945 --> 00:20:48,312
over at his place last week.

391
00:20:48,380 --> 00:20:49,380
Says who?

392
00:20:49,448 --> 00:20:50,492
Paul O'Neill.

393
00:20:50,516 --> 00:20:51,994
He was right outside the window.

394
00:20:52,018 --> 00:20:54,685
He heard the mayor accuse
you and Judge Franklin

395
00:20:54,754 --> 00:20:55,686
of betraying him.

396
00:20:55,755 --> 00:20:56,765
What did he mean by that?

397
00:20:56,789 --> 00:20:58,488
Judge Franklin and I were there

398
00:20:58,558 --> 00:21:00,301
to talk about a
run for the Senate.

399
00:21:00,325 --> 00:21:01,425
There wasn't any fight.

400
00:21:01,493 --> 00:21:03,005
Paul said there was.

401
00:21:03,029 --> 00:21:04,940
Paul O'Neill was
trespassing, all right?

402
00:21:04,964 --> 00:21:07,998
Maybe he, uh, made up the
story to justify what he did.

403
00:21:08,067 --> 00:21:09,645
Now, you're going
to have to leave.

404
00:21:09,669 --> 00:21:11,280
I'm busy. Really?

405
00:21:11,304 --> 00:21:13,304
I heard your
business is way off.

406
00:21:14,740 --> 00:21:16,139
Here... wear this.

407
00:21:16,208 --> 00:21:18,709
Leave it by the front
door on your way out.

408
00:21:31,024 --> 00:21:33,958
♪ ♪

409
00:21:49,141 --> 00:21:50,474
Hey, Mr. Chan.

410
00:21:50,543 --> 00:21:53,510
I'm, uh... I'm Ben Matlock,

411
00:21:53,579 --> 00:21:55,123
uh, Chris Reardon's lawyer.

412
00:21:55,147 --> 00:21:57,159
I saw your picture in the paper.

413
00:21:57,183 --> 00:21:58,582
Yes, well, I, uh, suppose

414
00:21:58,651 --> 00:22:00,684
you really have your
work cut out for you now,

415
00:22:00,753 --> 00:22:01,785
don't you, Mr. Matlock?

416
00:22:01,854 --> 00:22:02,898
How is that?

417
00:22:02,922 --> 00:22:03,922
You haven't heard.

418
00:22:05,024 --> 00:22:06,823
Well, the, uh, prosecution...

419
00:22:06,893 --> 00:22:09,393
they have an eyewitness,
Melissa Landis.

420
00:22:09,462 --> 00:22:11,695
Mayor Barelli's press aide?

421
00:22:11,764 --> 00:22:14,298
She was standing right beside
the mayor when he was shot.

422
00:22:14,367 --> 00:22:15,800
Saw the killer very clearly

423
00:22:15,868 --> 00:22:18,835
and, uh, she says it
was Chris Reardon.

424
00:22:18,904 --> 00:22:20,070
Huh.

425
00:22:20,138 --> 00:22:23,374
She say why she was
so long in coming forward?

426
00:22:23,442 --> 00:22:24,941
From what I understand,

427
00:22:25,011 --> 00:22:26,543
she was at Janet Barelli's side

428
00:22:26,612 --> 00:22:28,323
all the way up
until this morning.

429
00:22:28,347 --> 00:22:31,115
Well, if you'll excuse me,
Mr. Matlock, I am quite late.

430
00:22:31,183 --> 00:22:33,784
Uh, it was a
pleasure meeting you.

431
00:22:33,852 --> 00:22:35,786
Uh, Mr. Chan?

432
00:22:35,855 --> 00:22:40,423
Um, are you and, uh,
Jack Brennert good friends?

433
00:22:40,493 --> 00:22:43,060
We're, uh... we're
planning a, uh,

434
00:22:43,129 --> 00:22:45,073
memorial service
for the mayor, yes.

435
00:22:45,097 --> 00:22:48,265
Then, uh, you weren't
worried that, uh...

436
00:22:48,334 --> 00:22:50,267
I might see you here.

437
00:22:52,605 --> 00:22:55,165
Must have been my imagination.

438
00:23:16,662 --> 00:23:18,229
Hi. I'm here to
see Mrs. Barelli.

439
00:23:18,297 --> 00:23:19,497
I'm Michelle Thomas.

440
00:23:19,565 --> 00:23:21,398
I'm an attorney with
Ben Matlock's office.

441
00:23:21,467 --> 00:23:22,747
I don't care who you are.

442
00:23:22,801 --> 00:23:25,147
Mrs. Barelli is not ready
to talk to anyone yet.

443
00:23:25,171 --> 00:23:26,570
Melissa.

444
00:23:26,639 --> 00:23:27,639
It's all right.

445
00:23:27,707 --> 00:23:29,840
I'll talk with her.

446
00:23:29,909 --> 00:23:31,942
But I've got to go
back downtown.

447
00:23:32,011 --> 00:23:34,878
Well, go ahead, I'll be fine.

448
00:23:34,947 --> 00:23:37,648
Well, all right.

449
00:23:37,716 --> 00:23:40,483
But just let the phone ring,
because I left the machine on.

450
00:23:40,553 --> 00:23:42,019
Thank you, Melissa.

451
00:23:42,087 --> 00:23:43,387
Good-bye.

452
00:23:45,457 --> 00:23:46,891
I'm Michelle Thomas.

453
00:23:46,959 --> 00:23:48,626
It's nice to meet you.

454
00:23:48,694 --> 00:23:49,960
Shall we go in here?

455
00:23:52,498 --> 00:23:55,065
I appreciate your seeing me.

456
00:23:55,133 --> 00:23:56,767
Well, I'm only
doing what I believe

457
00:23:56,835 --> 00:23:58,535
my husband would've wanted.

458
00:23:58,604 --> 00:23:59,803
Please, have a seat.

459
00:23:59,872 --> 00:24:02,238
Thank you.

460
00:24:02,307 --> 00:24:05,375
Mrs. Barelli, I understand
your husband met with

461
00:24:05,445 --> 00:24:08,178
Stuart Franklin and Jack
Brennert last Friday night.

462
00:24:08,247 --> 00:24:09,713
That's correct.

463
00:24:09,782 --> 00:24:11,793
Can you tell me what
the meeting was about?

464
00:24:11,817 --> 00:24:12,850
Oh, I'm sorry.

465
00:24:12,919 --> 00:24:14,563
I just said hello
and then I left.

466
00:24:14,587 --> 00:24:18,288
Well, I-I understand that they
got into a violent argument.

467
00:24:18,357 --> 00:24:20,524
No, I don't think so.

468
00:24:20,592 --> 00:24:21,925
I was in the den reading.

469
00:24:21,994 --> 00:24:23,939
It's right next door;
I would've heard.

470
00:24:23,963 --> 00:24:25,562
Mrs. Barelli?

471
00:24:25,631 --> 00:24:26,931
May I see you for a moment?

472
00:24:26,999 --> 00:24:28,365
Yes, of course.

473
00:24:28,434 --> 00:24:29,666
Will you excuse us?

474
00:24:29,735 --> 00:24:32,136
Sure.

475
00:25:03,835 --> 00:25:05,313
Hello. No one is available

476
00:25:05,337 --> 00:25:06,537
to take your call right now.

477
00:25:06,605 --> 00:25:07,916
Please leave your
name and number

478
00:25:07,940 --> 00:25:09,060
at the sound of the beep

479
00:25:09,108 --> 00:25:10,919
and your call will be
returned. Thank you.

480
00:25:12,611 --> 00:25:15,345
Janice, this is Nicole Carlton.

481
00:25:15,414 --> 00:25:16,613
I got your message.

482
00:25:16,682 --> 00:25:18,259
If you really think
we should talk,

483
00:25:18,283 --> 00:25:20,617
let's do it over lunch tomorrow.

484
00:25:20,686 --> 00:25:22,752
The<i> Señor,</i> 1:00.

485
00:25:29,695 --> 00:25:32,730
Oh, there he is.

486
00:25:32,798 --> 00:25:34,643
The security guard
said we can go right up.

487
00:25:34,667 --> 00:25:36,411
Great. Tyler
Hudson, Paul O'Neill.

488
00:25:36,435 --> 00:25:37,712
Nice to meet you, Paul. Hey.

489
00:25:37,736 --> 00:25:40,804
Uh, Nicole Carlton
called Janice Barelli today.

490
00:25:40,873 --> 00:25:42,117
Who is Nicole Carlton?

491
00:25:42,141 --> 00:25:43,407
A model.

492
00:25:43,475 --> 00:25:45,355
The talk is she
and Mayor Barelli

493
00:25:45,411 --> 00:25:47,477
were very, very good friends.

494
00:25:47,546 --> 00:25:49,457
Oh, and the decanter
you said got broken

495
00:25:49,481 --> 00:25:50,892
during the fight in
the mayor's library...

496
00:25:50,916 --> 00:25:51,849
Yeah?

497
00:25:51,917 --> 00:25:53,484
It's not broken anymore.

498
00:25:53,552 --> 00:25:56,420
It's not crystal anymore either.

499
00:25:56,489 --> 00:25:58,222
Mrs. Barelli replaced it.

500
00:25:58,290 --> 00:26:01,558
Maybe she knows
more than she's saying.

501
00:26:04,630 --> 00:26:06,964
Somebody's coming.

502
00:26:11,270 --> 00:26:13,115
What do you suppose
they were fighting about?

503
00:26:13,139 --> 00:26:14,371
I don't know.

504
00:26:14,440 --> 00:26:18,341
Maybe we'll find out
in the mayor's office.

505
00:26:18,411 --> 00:26:20,422
It's O'Neill and this
is the only way out.

506
00:26:20,446 --> 00:26:22,379
It's no problem.

507
00:26:41,667 --> 00:26:42,677
Oh!

508
00:26:42,701 --> 00:26:45,035
It's Joseph Chan.
Call the paramedics.

509
00:26:47,640 --> 00:26:49,439
Oh, whoa!

510
00:26:52,578 --> 00:26:54,244
Hey!

511
00:27:03,922 --> 00:27:05,882
I'll go this way. All right.

512
00:28:02,815 --> 00:28:04,325
<i>I, uh...</i>

513
00:28:04,349 --> 00:28:06,950
came in to get some names
and addresses I needed

514
00:28:07,018 --> 00:28:09,887
out of the mayor's files
for the memorial service.

515
00:28:09,955 --> 00:28:12,455
As, uh... soon as I
unlocked the door,

516
00:28:12,524 --> 00:28:14,357
somebody came up from behind me,

517
00:28:14,426 --> 00:28:16,894
shoved me in and, uh,
hit me over the head.

518
00:28:16,962 --> 00:28:18,829
Did you get a look at him?

519
00:28:19,931 --> 00:28:20,997
No... no, I didn't.

520
00:28:21,066 --> 00:28:22,277
I did.

521
00:28:22,301 --> 00:28:23,778
He was a little under six feet.

522
00:28:23,802 --> 00:28:26,703
Looked exactly like the
guy who shot the mayor.

523
00:28:26,772 --> 00:28:30,207
Any idea why someone would
want to steal files from the mayor?

524
00:28:30,275 --> 00:28:32,041
Now, wait a minute.

525
00:28:32,110 --> 00:28:34,945
That implies that the
mayor had something to hide.

526
00:28:35,013 --> 00:28:36,973
It's not an unreasonable
assumption.

527
00:28:37,816 --> 00:28:38,816
Yes.

528
00:28:38,850 --> 00:28:41,551
Well, that is
simply not the case.

529
00:28:41,620 --> 00:28:44,654
Now, if you'll excuse
me, I'm going home.

530
00:28:44,723 --> 00:28:47,591
I'm very tired, and
my head is throbbing.

531
00:28:47,659 --> 00:28:49,593
Good night.

532
00:28:53,098 --> 00:28:54,909
You know, I'll be
the first to admit

533
00:28:54,933 --> 00:28:56,566
I don't like Joseph Chan.

534
00:28:56,635 --> 00:28:59,236
But if the mayor had
something to hide in his files,

535
00:28:59,304 --> 00:29:02,205
Joseph Chan would know
exactly what and where.

536
00:29:02,274 --> 00:29:03,274
Hmm.

537
00:29:03,309 --> 00:29:04,552
Now, maybe he and his friend

538
00:29:04,576 --> 00:29:06,021
came in here to get it.

539
00:29:06,045 --> 00:29:07,054
Maybe they heard us coming,

540
00:29:07,078 --> 00:29:08,590
and he had the other
guy knock him out.

541
00:29:08,614 --> 00:29:10,380
That devious?

542
00:29:10,448 --> 00:29:12,148
Oh, yeah.

543
00:29:13,252 --> 00:29:14,918
He does keep showing up.

544
00:29:14,986 --> 00:29:16,720
Mm-hmm.

545
00:29:20,092 --> 00:29:21,972
You want to get
something to eat?

546
00:29:22,928 --> 00:29:24,761
Uh, no, thanks.

547
00:29:25,964 --> 00:29:28,631
I'm planning on having
a big lunch tomorrow.

548
00:29:56,494 --> 00:29:57,906
Thank you for
joining me for lunch.

549
00:30:25,424 --> 00:30:27,068
Yeah, give me some
more. Come on, come on.

550
00:30:27,092 --> 00:30:28,124
Give me some more.

551
00:30:28,193 --> 00:30:29,193
Yeah, yeah.

552
00:30:29,227 --> 00:30:30,627
Like that, yeah.

553
00:30:30,696 --> 00:30:31,706
Yeah!

554
00:30:31,730 --> 00:30:32,730
Terrific.

555
00:30:32,764 --> 00:30:33,697
That's a wrap.

556
00:30:33,765 --> 00:30:35,898
Thanks, Nicole.

557
00:30:35,968 --> 00:30:37,634
Thank you.

558
00:30:39,638 --> 00:30:41,004
Ms. Carlton, hi.

559
00:30:41,073 --> 00:30:42,205
I'm Michelle Thomas.

560
00:30:42,274 --> 00:30:43,473
What's the ad for?

561
00:30:43,542 --> 00:30:44,719
Perfume. Oh.

562
00:30:44,743 --> 00:30:46,087
Do I know you? No.

563
00:30:46,111 --> 00:30:47,344
I'm an attorney.

564
00:30:47,413 --> 00:30:49,212
I'd like to talk to
you about the lunch

565
00:30:49,281 --> 00:30:51,514
you had today with Mrs. Barelli.

566
00:30:52,684 --> 00:30:54,462
First, she tries to pay me off,

567
00:30:54,486 --> 00:30:56,297
and now she sics
her attorneys on me.

568
00:30:56,321 --> 00:30:57,687
Is that it?

569
00:30:58,790 --> 00:31:01,236
Tell her this for me, lady.

570
00:31:01,260 --> 00:31:03,826
Tell her I have no
intention of telling anyone

571
00:31:03,895 --> 00:31:06,229
about my relationship
with her husband.

572
00:31:06,298 --> 00:31:07,997
Not because of her.

573
00:31:08,066 --> 00:31:09,733
Because of him.

574
00:31:09,801 --> 00:31:13,436
What Brian and I
shared was special.

575
00:31:13,505 --> 00:31:16,440
You think I want to see the
news media get hold of it?

576
00:31:16,508 --> 00:31:19,409
Watch them drag his
name through the mud?

577
00:31:19,478 --> 00:31:21,823
So, tell her to leave me alone.

578
00:31:21,847 --> 00:31:23,691
I don't work for Mrs. Barelli.

579
00:31:23,715 --> 00:31:27,250
I represent the man
accused of killing the mayor.

580
00:31:27,319 --> 00:31:28,885
So, uh, Mrs. Barelli

581
00:31:28,954 --> 00:31:31,132
had money in that
envelope at lunch.

582
00:31:31,156 --> 00:31:33,423
I think I've told you enough.

583
00:31:33,492 --> 00:31:35,558
I don't think so.

584
00:31:36,662 --> 00:31:38,862
Brian Barelli and
I were in love.

585
00:31:38,931 --> 00:31:40,564
As soon as he became senator,

586
00:31:40,632 --> 00:31:42,799
he was going to divorce
Janice and marry me.

587
00:31:42,868 --> 00:31:46,003
I don't know whether
he told her that or not,

588
00:31:46,071 --> 00:31:47,603
but if he did,

589
00:31:47,672 --> 00:31:49,906
you ought to be talking to her.

590
00:32:11,463 --> 00:32:13,307
You sure this is the
place you brought him?

591
00:32:13,331 --> 00:32:14,408
Positive.

592
00:32:14,432 --> 00:32:15,498
Thanks.

593
00:32:15,567 --> 00:32:17,234
He gave me a 20.

594
00:32:17,302 --> 00:32:18,935
Lucky for you.

595
00:32:34,019 --> 00:32:36,019
Hey, give me a beer.

596
00:32:43,729 --> 00:32:45,328
Looking for somebody?

597
00:32:46,431 --> 00:32:47,731
Yeah, actually, I am.

598
00:32:47,799 --> 00:32:49,099
Big guy.

599
00:32:49,167 --> 00:32:51,146
Long hair... wears it
kind of long in back.

600
00:32:51,170 --> 00:32:52,813
Fancy boots. You mean Eddie.

601
00:32:52,837 --> 00:32:53,770
Yeah. Too bad.

602
00:32:53,839 --> 00:32:54,971
You just missed him.

603
00:32:55,040 --> 00:32:56,818
- Any idea where he went?
- We pretty much

604
00:32:56,842 --> 00:32:58,987
keep our mouths shut around
here, know what I mean?

605
00:32:59,011 --> 00:33:01,211
What do I look like, a cop?

606
00:33:01,280 --> 00:33:02,345
Yeah.

607
00:33:03,548 --> 00:33:05,259
I owe Eddie some
money from a card game.

608
00:33:05,283 --> 00:33:06,403
He said he hung out here,

609
00:33:06,451 --> 00:33:08,262
so I thought I'd drop
by and pay him off.

610
00:33:08,286 --> 00:33:10,687
Leave the money with
me... I'll give it to him.

611
00:33:10,756 --> 00:33:12,722
Tell you what.

612
00:33:12,791 --> 00:33:15,292
Here's my name and phone number.

613
00:33:15,360 --> 00:33:17,527
You have Eddie call me.

614
00:33:26,137 --> 00:33:28,238
You're all mouth and
no brain, know that?

615
00:33:28,307 --> 00:33:29,472
What are you talking about?

616
00:33:29,541 --> 00:33:30,585
That guy didn't know Eddie.

617
00:33:30,609 --> 00:33:31,809
He didn't even know his name

618
00:33:31,843 --> 00:33:33,421
till you started
flapping your jaw.

619
00:33:33,445 --> 00:33:35,122
Take this over to Eddie's hotel.

620
00:33:35,146 --> 00:33:37,881
Tell him this guy
is looking for him.

621
00:33:39,051 --> 00:33:40,550
Do it now.

622
00:33:59,638 --> 00:34:01,571
♪ ♪

623
00:34:10,115 --> 00:34:12,593
I'm due in court in five
minutes, Mr. Matlock.

624
00:34:12,617 --> 00:34:14,261
You should've made
an appointment.

625
00:34:14,285 --> 00:34:15,719
I know and-and I tried,

626
00:34:15,788 --> 00:34:17,721
but you're a very busy man.

627
00:34:17,790 --> 00:34:18,988
If you're not in court,

628
00:34:19,057 --> 00:34:20,657
you're meeting
with civic leaders

629
00:34:20,726 --> 00:34:22,592
or organizing charity events.

630
00:34:22,661 --> 00:34:24,672
I've always served
my community, always.

631
00:34:24,696 --> 00:34:26,296
Well.

632
00:34:26,365 --> 00:34:29,899
Uh, you and
Brian Barelli were...

633
00:34:29,968 --> 00:34:31,901
pretty close friends, right?

634
00:34:33,004 --> 00:34:35,605
He was like a brother to me.

635
00:34:35,674 --> 00:34:38,642
Uh, I gather he helped secure,

636
00:34:38,710 --> 00:34:41,010
uh, your appointment
to the bench.

637
00:34:41,079 --> 00:34:42,445
Though the way I hear it,

638
00:34:42,514 --> 00:34:44,358
a lot of people were
surprised you took it.

639
00:34:44,382 --> 00:34:47,117
You were a popular councilman

640
00:34:47,185 --> 00:34:50,120
with a large constituency
on the South Side.

641
00:34:50,188 --> 00:34:53,623
They kind of expected you
to become mayor one day.

642
00:34:53,691 --> 00:34:55,625
Being a judge is more stable.

643
00:34:55,694 --> 00:34:57,994
And, evidently, a lot safer.

644
00:34:58,063 --> 00:34:59,996
Well.

645
00:35:00,065 --> 00:35:02,499
An odd coincidence,
though, wasn't it?

646
00:35:02,567 --> 00:35:03,733
What?

647
00:35:03,802 --> 00:35:05,969
Less than a month
after your appointment,

648
00:35:06,037 --> 00:35:10,039
the man who was mayor
resigned for reasons of health,

649
00:35:10,108 --> 00:35:14,210
then, uh, Brian Barelli
became mayor and not you.

650
00:35:18,250 --> 00:35:20,728
What are you getting at?

651
00:35:20,752 --> 00:35:24,187
Well, maybe your old
friend sandbagged you.

652
00:35:24,256 --> 00:35:27,524
Maybe he knew the man was ill.

653
00:35:27,592 --> 00:35:29,860
Got you appointed judge
just to kind of, you know,

654
00:35:29,928 --> 00:35:31,894
get you out of the way.

655
00:35:31,963 --> 00:35:33,129
That is ridiculous.

656
00:35:33,197 --> 00:35:34,631
Yeah, I guess, sure.

657
00:35:34,699 --> 00:35:37,099
I mean, if you felt you
had a score to settle

658
00:35:37,169 --> 00:35:40,102
with Mr. Barelli, why
wait around for three years

659
00:35:40,171 --> 00:35:41,371
to do anything about it?

660
00:35:41,439 --> 00:35:43,606
Of course, it
could explain that...

661
00:35:43,675 --> 00:35:45,074
terrible argument.

662
00:35:45,143 --> 00:35:46,209
Oh, here, let me help you.

663
00:35:46,278 --> 00:35:47,588
What argument? Yeah.

664
00:35:47,612 --> 00:35:49,245
Oh, the one out at his house.

665
00:35:49,314 --> 00:35:52,215
He said you betrayed
him, remember?

666
00:35:53,551 --> 00:35:55,185
That's funny. Imagine.

667
00:35:55,254 --> 00:35:57,186
Accusing you of betraying him.

668
00:36:00,425 --> 00:36:02,359
Golly, look at the time.

669
00:36:02,427 --> 00:36:03,593
I'm due in court myself.

670
00:36:03,662 --> 00:36:05,694
Maybe I'll see you there.

671
00:36:05,764 --> 00:36:07,697
You never can tell.

672
00:36:37,796 --> 00:36:39,729
♪ ♪

673
00:37:09,661 --> 00:37:11,594
♪ ♪

674
00:37:16,668 --> 00:37:18,601
Hear you're looking for me, huh?

675
00:37:18,670 --> 00:37:21,871
Not anymore. So,
you carry a piece.

676
00:37:21,940 --> 00:37:23,172
You a cop?

677
00:37:23,241 --> 00:37:24,607
No, just paranoid.

678
00:37:34,920 --> 00:37:37,086
<i>Lots of senators get divorced.</i>

679
00:37:37,155 --> 00:37:40,256
Not if they want to
become president.

680
00:37:40,324 --> 00:37:41,424
Oh.

681
00:37:41,493 --> 00:37:43,426
Still, people might
think you were trying

682
00:37:43,495 --> 00:37:45,761
to buy Miss Carlton
off as a way of...

683
00:37:45,830 --> 00:37:49,299
covering up the fact that you
had a motive for killing him.

684
00:37:49,368 --> 00:37:51,134
That's not true!

685
00:37:51,202 --> 00:37:53,269
That's how it could look.

686
00:37:53,338 --> 00:37:54,970
You don't intend

687
00:37:55,039 --> 00:37:57,340
to make these
accusations public, do you?

688
00:37:57,409 --> 00:38:01,177
Chris Reardon is
innocent, Mrs. Barelli.

689
00:38:01,246 --> 00:38:04,748
Ben Matlock and
I intend prove that.

690
00:38:04,816 --> 00:38:07,083
If we have to make
all this public, we will.

691
00:38:07,152 --> 00:38:08,751
Is that a threat?

692
00:38:08,820 --> 00:38:10,353
It's a strong suggestion

693
00:38:10,422 --> 00:38:12,021
that you be honest with us.

694
00:38:12,090 --> 00:38:14,023
I have been honest.

695
00:38:15,126 --> 00:38:17,927
The argument your
husband had in here

696
00:38:17,996 --> 00:38:19,929
with Franklin and Brennert,

697
00:38:19,998 --> 00:38:22,098
what was it about?

698
00:38:24,202 --> 00:38:26,836
I don't know.

699
00:38:28,940 --> 00:38:31,875
I think you do.

700
00:38:34,346 --> 00:38:36,313
I'd like you to leave now.

701
00:38:36,381 --> 00:38:37,747
If you really

702
00:38:37,816 --> 00:38:39,749
believed in
justice, Mrs. Barelli,

703
00:38:39,818 --> 00:38:42,118
you'd help us find the
man who killed him.

704
00:39:06,311 --> 00:39:08,243
Mr. Matlock.

705
00:39:08,312 --> 00:39:10,245
Where have you been?

706
00:39:10,314 --> 00:39:12,748
I caught up with the guy
we were chasing last night.

707
00:39:12,817 --> 00:39:14,584
Actually, he caught up with me.

708
00:39:14,653 --> 00:39:16,419
Knocked me out with my own gun,

709
00:39:16,488 --> 00:39:18,421
which he then stole.

710
00:39:18,489 --> 00:39:20,134
You were carrying a gun?

711
00:39:20,158 --> 00:39:21,624
I thought I might
run into trouble.

712
00:39:21,693 --> 00:39:24,460
You suckered me
into taking this case,

713
00:39:24,529 --> 00:39:26,028
but now it's my case.

714
00:39:26,097 --> 00:39:29,265
I'll tell you when, where and
if you're gonna get in trouble.

715
00:39:29,334 --> 00:39:30,700
I'm already in trouble.

716
00:39:30,769 --> 00:39:32,413
I don't have a
permit for the gun.

717
00:39:32,437 --> 00:39:35,405
You've got to go find that gun.

718
00:39:35,473 --> 00:39:36,572
I can't.

719
00:39:36,641 --> 00:39:38,408
I've been subpoenaed.

720
00:39:40,512 --> 00:39:44,280
What was the man who pointed
the gun at you wearing, Mr. O'Neill?

721
00:39:44,349 --> 00:39:47,283
Jeans, an army
jacket, sunglasses,

722
00:39:47,352 --> 00:39:49,163
and a Chicago Cubs baseball cap.

723
00:39:49,187 --> 00:39:50,620
Now you accompanied
Sgt. Marshall

724
00:39:50,689 --> 00:39:52,399
to the defendant's
apartment about an hour later,

725
00:39:52,423 --> 00:39:53,856
correct? Yes.

726
00:39:53,925 --> 00:39:55,169
When the defendant arrived home,

727
00:39:55,193 --> 00:39:57,627
what was he wearing?

728
00:39:57,695 --> 00:39:59,629
Jeans, an army
jacket, sunglasses,

729
00:39:59,697 --> 00:40:01,597
and a Chicago Cubs baseball cap.

730
00:40:01,666 --> 00:40:04,534
But... That's all, Mr. O'Neill.

731
00:40:04,602 --> 00:40:08,504
Defense wish to cross? Please.

732
00:40:11,576 --> 00:40:14,110
Uh, the man who
pointed the gun at you,

733
00:40:14,178 --> 00:40:16,479
when you were chasing
him... Was it Chris Reardon?

734
00:40:16,548 --> 00:40:17,981
No, it was not.

735
00:40:18,049 --> 00:40:20,783
Melissa Landis said
it was Chris Reardon.

736
00:40:20,852 --> 00:40:23,252
It was a guy dressed up
to look like Chris Reardon.

737
00:40:23,321 --> 00:40:24,754
What makes you so sure?

738
00:40:24,823 --> 00:40:26,500
Well, for one thing,
the man who shot

739
00:40:26,524 --> 00:40:29,459
Mayor Barelli tore his
sleeve making his getaway.

740
00:40:29,527 --> 00:40:31,472
The jacket Chris Reardon
was wearing an hour later

741
00:40:31,496 --> 00:40:32,496
was not torn.

742
00:40:32,530 --> 00:40:33,907
And for another thing,

743
00:40:33,931 --> 00:40:35,443
the letter I got tipping me off

744
00:40:35,467 --> 00:40:37,967
to the assassination was
not written by Chris Reardon.

745
00:40:38,036 --> 00:40:40,470
An expert said it was
typed on his typewriter.

746
00:40:40,538 --> 00:40:41,818
It was.

747
00:40:41,873 --> 00:40:44,273
But whenever Chris wrote
to me about Mayor Barelli,

748
00:40:44,342 --> 00:40:46,275
he always misspelled his name.

749
00:40:46,344 --> 00:40:47,988
Instead of two
"L's" and one "R",

750
00:40:48,012 --> 00:40:50,424
he always used two
"R's" and one "L".

751
00:40:50,448 --> 00:40:53,383
Hmm.

752
00:40:53,451 --> 00:40:57,220
Is there any other
reason you think

753
00:40:57,289 --> 00:41:01,257
the assassin was someone
other than Chris Reardon?

754
00:41:01,326 --> 00:41:02,625
Yeah.

755
00:41:02,694 --> 00:41:06,095
To a lot of people Chris
Reardon is a royal pain in the...

756
00:41:06,164 --> 00:41:07,597
I understand.

757
00:41:07,665 --> 00:41:09,098
He protests.

758
00:41:09,167 --> 00:41:10,099
He demonstrates.

759
00:41:10,167 --> 00:41:11,667
He makes noise.

760
00:41:11,736 --> 00:41:14,837
But that's because he believes
this can be a better place.

761
00:41:14,906 --> 00:41:17,840
He sees what's wrong,
he doesn't give up.

762
00:41:17,909 --> 00:41:20,776
But he doesn't kill people.

763
00:41:20,845 --> 00:41:21,845
He's loud.

764
00:41:21,879 --> 00:41:22,890
He bothers people.

765
00:41:22,914 --> 00:41:24,124
So there's a lot of people

766
00:41:24,148 --> 00:41:25,859
that'd like to
believe that he did it.

767
00:41:25,883 --> 00:41:26,928
But I've talked with him,

768
00:41:26,952 --> 00:41:28,885
I've written about
him, I know him.

769
00:41:28,954 --> 00:41:31,086
Chris Reardon
couldn't commit murder.

770
00:41:34,092 --> 00:41:36,592
Thank you.

771
00:41:39,530 --> 00:41:41,797
Having heard

772
00:41:41,866 --> 00:41:44,967
the testimony presented
by the prosecution,

773
00:41:45,036 --> 00:41:48,137
this court finds reason
to believe the crime

774
00:41:48,206 --> 00:41:51,140
of murder in the first
degree has been committed.

775
00:41:52,543 --> 00:41:55,478
The defendant shall
be bound over for trial

776
00:41:55,547 --> 00:42:02,251
on June 7, in district
court two at 9:00 AM.

777
00:42:02,320 --> 00:42:04,200
Court adjourned.

778
00:42:06,257 --> 00:42:08,191
Round one for the bad guys.

779
00:42:08,259 --> 00:42:11,194
But you put up a
pretty good fight.

780
00:42:13,330 --> 00:42:16,343
- Uh, Ben...
- Hmm?

781
00:42:16,367 --> 00:42:18,300
Oh.

782
00:42:23,341 --> 00:42:25,275
Janice Barelli, Ben Matlock.

783
00:42:25,343 --> 00:42:26,809
Hello. Hello.

784
00:42:26,878 --> 00:42:29,312
There was an
argument that night,

785
00:42:29,380 --> 00:42:31,447
and Brian was very upset.

786
00:42:34,319 --> 00:42:36,252
And you were right, Miss Thomas.

787
00:42:36,321 --> 00:42:37,920
Whatever upset Brian,

788
00:42:37,989 --> 00:42:40,456
I don't think he would have
wanted it swept under the rug.

789
00:42:42,427 --> 00:42:44,260
You didn't hear anything?

790
00:42:46,364 --> 00:42:47,963
Ask Joseph Chan.

791
00:42:48,032 --> 00:42:49,152
He arranged that meeting.

792
00:42:49,200 --> 00:42:51,078
He had to have known
what was going on.

793
00:42:51,102 --> 00:42:53,202
It was nice to meet you.

794
00:42:53,271 --> 00:42:55,038
Bye.

795
00:42:55,106 --> 00:42:56,917
I've known Joseph
Chan a long time.

796
00:42:56,941 --> 00:42:59,501
Let me check this out.

797
00:43:01,479 --> 00:43:03,412
♪ ♪

798
00:43:41,619 --> 00:43:43,352
Chan?

799
00:44:13,385 --> 00:44:15,318
♪ ♪

800
00:44:16,388 --> 00:44:19,322
Ben Matlock, please.

801
00:44:19,391 --> 00:44:20,990
Ben, it's Paul.

802
00:44:21,059 --> 00:44:22,158
Chan's been killed.

803
00:44:22,227 --> 00:44:24,338
Shot through the chest.

804
00:44:24,362 --> 00:44:27,296
Looks like it happened
just before I got here.

805
00:44:27,365 --> 00:44:28,731
Okay.

806
00:44:33,638 --> 00:44:36,506
Yeah, reporting a murder
at 2733 Woodman Avenue.

807
00:45:05,236 --> 00:45:07,937
♪ ♪

808
00:45:19,417 --> 00:45:21,350
What?

809
00:45:21,419 --> 00:45:22,785
Hold it!

810
00:45:22,854 --> 00:45:23,986
Freeze!

811
00:45:40,438 --> 00:45:42,371
Oh, uh, Sergeant.

812
00:45:43,441 --> 00:45:44,919
Matlock, right? Yes.

813
00:45:44,943 --> 00:45:46,186
Reardon's lawyer? Yes.

814
00:45:46,210 --> 00:45:47,976
Uh, is Paul O'Neill around?

815
00:45:48,045 --> 00:45:49,256
Not anymore.

816
00:45:49,280 --> 00:45:50,280
Where'd he go?

817
00:45:50,315 --> 00:45:52,247
That's what we'd like to know.

818
00:45:52,316 --> 00:45:54,583
A couple of my men
saw him leaving the scene

819
00:45:54,651 --> 00:45:56,285
just as they arrived.

820
00:45:56,354 --> 00:45:57,453
Had a gun on him.

821
00:45:57,522 --> 00:45:58,754
What?

822
00:45:58,822 --> 00:46:01,457
I'm putting a warrant
out for his arrest.

823
00:46:01,526 --> 00:46:03,292
Too bad you're taken.

824
00:46:03,361 --> 00:46:06,061
'Cause he's gonna need
one hell of a good lawyer.

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
